  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "A one-run shutout on the road, built from the bottom up. Pitching depth and just enough contact to hold the line.",
    "what_this_means": "Wilkinson's early exit opened the door for the bullpen to carry the game, and it did. Houser threw five clean innings, Smith locked down the final two with five strikeouts, and no one had to carry the whole load. That's the formula when the lineup can't generate much: the back end holds tight, and one run is enough. Adames went 3-for-4. He was the separator, the only hitter who consistently put the ball in play. But this wasn't about him alone. This was about a structure that worked: minimize damage, execute late relief, survive. With a .248 team average and runners left stranded in three of four RISP situations, the Giants won anyway. That's not sustainable, and the math gets tighter every time. One run holds today. It won't always.",
